  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "A point is chosen uniformly in a unit square. Find the probability that the point is closer to a corner than the centre of the square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe four corner regions are congruent right triangles whose combined area is half the square.",
      "worked": "Place the square at $0\\le x,y\\le1$, with centre $C=(\\frac12,\\frac12)$. The perpendicular bisector between $C$ and the corner $(0,0)$ satisfies\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+y^2&=(x-\\tfrac12)^2+(y-\\tfrac12)^2\\\\x+y&=\\tfrac12.\\end{align*}$$\nPoints closer to this corner lie in the right triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(\\frac12,0)$ and $(0,\\frac12)$. Each of the four corners gives a congruent triangle, and their interiors do not overlap.\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{corner regions}}&=4\\times\\tfrac12\\times\\tfrac12\\times\\tfrac12\\\\A_{\\text{corner regions}}&=\\tfrac12\\\\\\Pr(E)&=\\frac{\\tfrac12}{1}\\\\\\Pr(E)&=\\tfrac12.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
